    "title": "1. The big idea on one page",
    "summary": "Smart Money Concepts (SMC) teach you to read the footprints of big institutions on a price chart, so you trade with them and not against them.",
    "content": "Smart Money Concepts (SMC) teach you to read the footprints of big institutions on a price chart, so you trade with them and not against them.\n\nSmart Money means banks, hedge funds and other traders with very large portfolios. Their orders are so large that they cannot just click \"buy\" or \"sell\". They must hide their buying and selling inside the moves of the market. Smart Money Concepts give you a way to see those moves.\n\n> **The one sentence to remember: retail traders put their stop losses in obvious places. Those stop losses become the liquidity that Smart Money uses to fill its big orders.**\n\n### What this chapter teaches\nThe lesson has four goals. Each goal is a section group in this chapter.\n\n\n| Goal | Where in this chapter |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| 1. What Smart Money is and how it shapes the market | Sections 2 and 3 |\n| 2. The difference between institutions and retail traders | Section 3 |\n| 3. The core tools: liquidity, market structure, order blocks, fair value gaps | Sections 4, 5 and 12 |\n| 4. How to recognise manipulation: stop hunts, fake breakouts, liquidity grabs | Sections 6, 7 and 8 |\n\nThe rest of the chapter shows a real chart example (Section 9), the entry rules (Section 10), risk and reward (Section 11), time frames (Section 13) and your homework (Section 14).\n\n\n### How this series works\nThe lessons come from a live stream with two people: Roman, who teaches, and a host who asks the questions a beginner would ask.\n\nBoth say clearly: they are not experts. They are learning as they go and are sharing what they learned in the past few weeks.\n\nThey do not tell you to risk your money. First comes theory, then paper trading, and later live trading with real money.\n\nOrder of the course (from the stream): this lesson gives the overview. The next lesson teaches how to find structural swing points, and after that, how to read market structure (bullish or bearish).\n\n\n### Map of the chapter",
