    "id": "ch1-sec4",
    "num": "04",
    "title": "4. Liquidity: why price goes where the stop losses are",
    "summary": "Liquidity means orders waiting at a price level, and without liquidity Smart Money cannot fill its orders.",
    "content": "Liquidity means orders waiting at a price level, and without liquidity Smart Money cannot fill its orders.\n\nThe main rule: Smart Money moves the price into levels of high liquidity.\n\nWhen Smart Money wants to buy, it moves the price to a level where retail traders are selling.\n\nWhen Smart Money wants to sell, it moves the price up to a level where retail traders are buying.\n\nRoman's key sentence: \"price moves from liquidity to liquidity.\" It takes liquidity from the long traders, then goes and takes liquidity from the short traders, and moves up and down between key liquidity levels.\n\n\n### Where the liquidity sits\nUnder every swing low there is a pool of sell orders. These are the stop losses of traders who bought near the low.\n\nAbove every swing high there is a pool of buy orders. These are the stop losses of traders who sold near the high, and the orders of breakout traders.\n\nThe names used later in the course: sell-side liquidity (below lows) and buy-side liquidity (above highs).\n\nWhy it happens: many retail traders treat these levels as support and resistance, and they all put their stop losses on the other side of them.\n\nA stop loss on a long position is a sell order. A stop loss on a short position is a buy order. (Roman says these are stop limit orders, and the host asks if they can be stop market orders too.)\n\n\n### The host's own story\nThe host usually puts his stop loss under a swing low, because he thinks that if the level breaks, price will go down further.\n\nRoman's answer: that can very well be a false breakout. Big players sweep the liquidity, take your stop loss, and then push the price the other way.\n\n> **Important: it is not always a sweep. Sometimes the price really does push down. Later in the course you will learn how to find the strong swings that are less likely to break.**\n\n### You cannot see this in the order book\nA viewer asked how to see these orders in the order book. Answer: it is very hard, because stop orders are hidden. They only appear in the order book when the stop price is triggered.\n\nSo Smart Money Concepts only look at price action on the chart, not at the order book. You look for the clues the orders leave behind.\n\n\n### Where Smart Money gets its volume\nA viewer asked if big amounts are bought directly from miners. Roman: you can, but you can also buy from retail traders below the market price. You move the market to a good position, then sweep up all the stop-loss orders and collect very large volume.",
