    "id": "ch1-sec10",
    "num": "10",
    "title": "10. Entry rules and the questions viewers asked",
    "summary": "The entry order never goes into the liquidity zone itself: you wait for price to arrive, then wait for a confirmation on a lower time frame.",
    "content": "The entry order never goes into the liquidity zone itself: you wait for price to arrive, then wait for a confirmation on a lower time frame.\n\n\n### The entry rules\nDo not place orders beforehand. Roman: \"that's not how smart money works.\" There are many swing points where you could place a long order, and doing so is not correct.\n\nWait until price comes into the level (the POI). Place an alert there so you do not need to watch the chart.\n\nWhen price arrives, switch to a lower time frame. If you started on the daily chart, use the 1-hour chart.\n\nOn the lower time frame, look for this pattern: price goes down and takes out the liquidity, and then you see a break of structure (a shift in market structure).\n\nOnly after the shift do you enter.\n\nThe most basic and conservative entry is a limit order around the break of structure (the break and retest). Roman says you can enter here and be safe.\n\nThere are other cases, for example entering lower, or entering with a market order as soon as a candle closes above the level. These are covered later.\n\n\n### It is not guaranteed\nPrice can always sweep again after your entry. After a first sweep there can be a second one at another swing point. A third one is very rare.\n\nIf you are stopped out, you may see a new reversal and enter again. This time it may work.\n\n\n| Question | Answer from the lesson |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Can I see these stop orders in the order book? | No. They are hidden until triggered. Smart Money Concepts read only the chart. |\n| Is it a fake or a real breakout? Should I DCA down? | You cannot know in advance. Do not buy at the break. Wait for the shift on the lower time frame. You usually do  not  DCA. |\n| Why no DCA? | This strategy is mostly used on  futures . On futures you do not DCA: you use stop losses. This also lets you collect statistics and see your win rate. On spot you can try DCA, but usually it is not done. |\n| Can I see the volume or use other indicators? | Volume and RSI are only extra confirmation (see Section 9). |\n| How do I trade this with a trading tool? | Later in the series. First theory, then paper trading, then live trading with real money. |\n| Should I risk my money yet? 100x leverage? | The hosts joked about this. Do not take it as advice: they say they are still learning. |\n\n\n### The style of trading\nThe host sums it up: it is a very risk-based style. You must be careful about your success rate, and each setup must have a high enough risk-to-reward ratio to cover the losses, especially on a losing streak. There will be losses. Nothing is guaranteed.",
