    "id": "ch1-sec11",
    "num": "11",
    "title": "11. Risk-to-reward and win rate",
    "summary": "You can be wrong most of the time and still make money, if your winners are bigger than your losers.",
    "content": "You can be wrong most of the time and still make money, if your winners are bigger than your losers.\n\n\n### The numbers from the lesson\nRoman talked to traders who use this strategy. Their win rate is usually around 50%, sometimes 45%.\n\nTheir risk-to-reward ratio (RR) is usually at least 1 to 3.\n\nA viewer said that at 3:1 you only need to be right 30% of the time. The host's answer: 30% is about break-even, so you need to be a bit better. But a 40% win rate is still profitable. You are wrong most of the time and still make money, and the host says that can only happen in trading.\n\nRoman adds that 1 to 3 is actually conservative. Many traders use 1 to 5 or higher and still get about a 40% win rate.\n\n\n### What RR means\nYou risk 1 unit (the distance from your entry to your stop loss).\n\nYou aim to win 3 units (the distance from your entry to your take profit).\n\n\"1 to 3\" means that one winner pays for three losers.\n\n\n### Letting profits run\n\n### The host says that tools with these features can raise the RR of a setup\nMultiple take profits.\n\nA trailing take profit.\n\nMoving the stop loss to break-even after the first take profit.\n\nThe first take profit makes sure the position is profitable. Then the extra take profits and the trailing one squeeze the most out of the trade. The host says this is how big traders make the most money: by letting their trades ride.\n\nA worked example (the stream started this calculation but did not finish it)\n\n\n### Take 100 trades, a 45% win rate, a 1:3 RR and a risk of $100 per trade\n\n| Category | Trades | Result per trade | Total |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Winners | 45 | +$300 | +$13,500 |\n| Losers | 55 | -$100 | -$5,500 |\n| Net | 100 |  | +$8,000 |\n\nThis ignores fees and slippage, which reduce the result.\n\nExtra note (not from the video): the exact break-even win rate is 1 divided by (1 + RR). For RR 3 it is 25%, for RR 5 it is about 17%, and for RR 1 it is 50%. Fees move the line up a little, so the host's 30% is a safer rule of thumb.\n\n\n### Break-even win rate\n\n### The worked example in a drawing",
