    "id": "ch1-sec15",
    "num": "15",
    "title": "15. One-page summary: key terms, rules, mistakes, memory tricks",
    "summary": "Retail stop losses are the liquidity Smart Money needs.",
    "content": "### Key terms\n\n| Term | Meaning in this chapter |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Smart Money | Banks, hedge funds and traders with huge portfolios |\n| Retail traders | Individual traders with small accounts |\n| Liquidity | Orders waiting at a price: stop losses, pending orders, breakout orders |\n| Buy-side liquidity | Buy orders above swing highs |\n| Sell-side liquidity | Sell orders below swing lows |\n| Swing high / swing low | A turning point on the chart (made precise in Chapter 2) |\n| Market structure | The pattern of swing highs and lows (higher highs and higher lows in an uptrend) |\n| Stop hunt | Price runs into a level full of stop losses to trigger them |\n| Fake breakout | A break of a level that reverses and traps breakout traders |\n| Liquidity grab / sweep | A quick move beyond a level (often a wick) that collects orders |\n| Shift in market structure | A break of the last lower high (or higher low) on the lower time frame, after a sweep |\n| Point of interest (POI) | A zone where you look for a reversal: FVG, order block, liquidity |\n| Fair value gap (FVG) | A gap between the first and third candle of a three-candle move |\n| Order block (OB) | The last opposite candle before a strong move |\n| Internal / external liquidity | Liquidity inside a range / outside it |\n| HTF / LTF | Higher time frame (direction) and lower time frame (entry) |\n| RR | Risk-to-reward ratio |\n| Wyckoff distribution | Old idea: big players sell to the crowd during a rise, then push price down |\n\n\n### The rules in one list\nRetail stop losses are the liquidity Smart Money needs.\n\nPrice moves from liquidity to liquidity.\n\nEvery swing low has sell orders below it. Every swing high has buy orders above it.\n\nSmart Money moves price into liquidity first, and then makes its real move.\n\nDo not enter at the level. Wait for the sweep and then the shift on the lower time frame.\n\nThe basic entry is the sweep, then the break of structure, then the retest.\n\nAlways pair a high time frame with a matching low time frame.\n\nUse stop losses, aim for at least 1 to 3, and do not DCA on futures.\n\nVolume and RSI are extra confirmation only.\n\nHigher time frames are more reliable than lower ones.\n\n\n### Common mistakes\nBuying at a swing low with the stop loss right under it.\n\nTreating every level break as a real breakout.\n\nPlacing orders in the zone before any confirmation.\n\nMarking swing points that are not structural.\n\nTrading against the direction of the structure.\n\nUsing a daily chart with a 5-minute chart.\n\nUsing very high leverage before you are consistent.\n\nTaking every FVG or order block as a trade.\n\n\n### Checklist before an entry\n\n### ☐ Do I know the direction (structure) on the high time frame?\n\n### ☐ Is price inside a point of interest?\n\n### ☐ Did price sweep liquidity on the lower time frame?\n\n### ☐ Did the structure shift, and did price retest?\n\n### ☐ Is my stop loss placed and is my RR at least 1 to 3?\n\n### ☐ Is my risk per trade small?\n\n### Memory tricks\n\n### \"Your stop is their entry.\"\n\n### \"Sweep, shift, retest, enter.\"\n\n### \"Liquidity to liquidity.\"\n\n### \"High time frame for direction, low time frame for entry.\"\n\n### \"Wait for the alert, then look.\"",
